HummingLinearKernel called prepare_humming_linear_layer_config with the default allow_input_schema_fallback=True, and
check_and_fallback_input_schema then returns a 16-bit input schema for 8-bit activations on groupwise weights
("prefer wna16 for groupwise weight or groupwise input"). An int8 activation config that a checkpoint declares
explicitly (CompressedTensorsWNA8Int) was therefore served as W4A16, silently. On the int4 g128 Qwen3.8-27B
checkpoint at 64 concurrent requests on an RTX 3090 the int8 path is the faster one: 1,119 tok/s against 790 at
128/512, for +1.04% perplexity. A layer with an input quant config now passes allow_input_schema_fallback=False,
so the schema it asked for is the one built (an incompatible pair raises in Humming instead of falling back), and
the first layer config built for each activation dtype is logged ("Humming layer config: a_dtype=int8").

Kind: fix
What: the Humming linear kernel honours an explicit int8 input quant config (input-schema fallback off for it),
  where Humming's groupwise heuristic served it 16-bit, and logs the first layer config per activation dtype;
  INT8_ACT=int8 rides on this and wna8-int-humming-kernel
Upstream: none yet; upstream main warns on that fallback and forbids it under VLLM_STRICT_QUANT_SCHEME=1 (#59460)
Cut-against: 0.34.0
Retires-when: upstream honours an explicit input quant config

